If you don't want the stream to terminate on timeout and instead want the missing data to be null, you can use the `checkpointing` feature in Kafka Streams.

With checkpointing, Kafka Streams periodically saves the current state of the data being processed to a checkpoint store. This allows the stream processing to continue even if the consumer encounters a timeout error.

Here's an example code snippet in Java that demonstrates how to do this:
```java
Properties props = new Properties();
props.put(StreamsConfig.APPLICATION_ID_CONFIG, "my-streams-app");
props.put(StreamsConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "localhost:9092");
props.put(StreamsConfig.DEFAULT_KEY_SERDE_CLASS_CONFIG, Serdes.String().getClass().getName());
props.put(StreamsConfig.DEFAULT_VALUE_SERDE_CLASS_CONFIG, Serdes.String().getClass().getName());

// Set the checkpoint interval to 1 second
props.put(StreamsConfig检查点间隔.配置, 1000);

StreamsBuilder builder = new StreamsBuilder();
KStream<String, String> source = builder.stream("my-input-topic");

KTable<String, Long> aggregated = source.groupByKey()
        .count(Materialized.<String, Long, KeyValueStore<Bytes, byte[]>>as("my-aggregated-store"));

aggregated.toStream().foreach((key, value) -> System.out.println(key + ": " + value));

KafkaStreams streams = new KafkaStreams(builder.build(), props);

streams.start();

// Wait for the stream processing to finish
streams.cleanUp();

// Commit the current offsets manually
streams.consumer().commitSync();

streams.close();
```
In this example, we set the checkpoint interval to 1 second by setting the `checkpoint.interval.ms` configuration property. This means that Kafka Streams will save the current state of the data being processed every 1 second.

If a timeout error occurs, the stream processing will continue with the last available data instead of terminating.
