	* Configure Gunicorn in a `gunicorn.conf.py` file (e.g., `gunicorn.conf.py` in the same directory as your `app.py` file).
	* Run Gunicorn: `gunicorn app:app -b 0.0.0.0:8080`
	* This will run your app in a separate process and bind it to `0.0.0.0:8080`.
2. **Option 2: Use Nginx as a Reverse Proxy**
	* Configure Nginx to act as a reverse proxy and forward requests to your Gunicorn instance (or another WSGI server).
	* Create a new file in `/etc/nginx/sites-available/` (e.g., `site-available.py`) with the following content:
```bash
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name localhost;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    }
}
```
* Create a symbolic link to make the new site available:
`sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/site-available.py /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/`
* Restart Nginx: `sudo service nginx restart`
* Run your Gunicorn instance with `gunicorn app:app -b 0.0.0.0:8080`

